    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="blog-section" id="mistake">
    <h2>Common Board Foot Mistakes</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Dividing by 12 when length is in inches (should divide by 144).</li>
      <li>Using nominal dimensions when the supplier prices by actual dimensions, or vice versa.</li>
      <li>Forgetting to multiply the per-board result by the number of boards in the order.</li>
      <li>Confusing board feet (volume) with square feet (area) or linear feet (length).</li>
      <li>Not adding a waste allowance before placing the lumber order.</li>
      <li>Assuming all suppliers measure the same way — always confirm the measurement standard.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div class="blog-section" id="faq">
    <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <% const _faqs = [["How do I calculate board feet of lumber?", "Multiply thickness (inches) × width (inches) × length (feet), then divide by 12.<br><strong>Formula: T × W × L / 12 = Board Feet</strong>"], ["How do you calculate bd ft?", "\"Bd ft\" is the abbreviation for board feet. The formula is the same: T (in) × W (in) × L (ft) A 12. If measuring length in inches, use / 144."], ["How many board feet are in a 2×6×10 board?", "Using nominal dimensions: 2 × 6 × 10 / 12 = <strong>10 board feet</strong>."], ["Is a board foot the same as a cubic foot?", "No. One board foot = 144 cubic inches. One cubic foot = 1,728 cubic inches. Therefore <strong>1 cubic foot = 12 board feet</strong>."], ["Does board-foot pricing include waste?", "Usually, board-foot pricing refers only to the measured volume of lumber. Waste, defects, unusable sections, delivery fees, and cutting loss are typically handled separately — either by the supplier or as a project allowance you add yourself."], ["What is the difference between nominal and actual lumber dimensions?", "A nominal 2×4 actually measures about 1.5 in × 3.5 in after drying and surfacing. Always confirm with your supplier whether board-foot pricing uses nominal or actual dimensions — this significantly affects the true price per board."]]; _faqs.forEach(function(f) { %>
